# Cold-Blooded vs. Warm-Blooded Animals

## Cold-Blooded (Ectothermic)
- **Body temperature** fluctuates with the environment
- Rely on external sources (sun, warm surfaces) to regulate body heat
- Examples: reptiles, amphibians, fish, insects
- **Advantages**: Need less food/energy to survive, can go longer without eating
- **Disadvantages**: Become sluggish in cold temperatures, less active in unfavorable weather

## Warm-Blooded (Endothermic)
- **Body temperature** stays relatively constant regardless of surroundings
- Generate heat internally through metabolism
- Examples: mammals, birds
- **Advantages**: Can stay active in various climates and temperatures, consistent energy levels
- **Disadvantages**: Require more food to fuel their metabolism, must expend energy to regulate temperature (shivering, sweating, panting)

## Key Scientific Terms
- **Ectotherm** = "outside heat" (cold-blooded)
- **Endotherm** = "inside heat" (warm-blooded)

## Quick Comparison

| Feature | Cold-Blooded | Warm-Blooded |
|---------|--------------|--------------|
| Heat source | External | Internal (metabolism) |
| Body temp | Variable | Constant |
| Food needs | Lower | Higher |
| Activity in cold | Reduced/dormant | Maintained |
| Energy efficiency | High | Lower |

